      name: 'Musonius Rufus',
      role: 'TEACHER OF THE TEACHER',
      body: `The Stoic philosopher who taught Epictetus during the years when Epictetus was still a slave. Musonius is one of the great figures of imperial Stoicism in his own right, exiled twice — by Nero and Vespasian — for his refusal to flatter, returning each time to teach in Rome. Surviving fragments of his lectures show the same austere practicality that runs through the Manual, including the unusual-for-his-time argument that women should be educated in philosophy on the same terms as men. The intellectual line — Musonius to Epictetus, Epictetus through Arrian to Marcus Aurelius — is one of the cleanest pedagogical chains in the history of philosophy, and it explains how a practical, classroom-tested Stoicism made it from the schools of late Republican Rome to the bedside of an emperor a hundred years later.`,
